policies.policy:
This policy meets HCS 281 (8/15), p.14, item B2(a) — Job Description: duties and responsibilities, and item B2(b) — Job Description: lines of supervision (supervision given and to whom; supervision received and from whom), for the Affiliated Home Care Aide classification.

WHO IS AN AFFILIATED AIDE: An affiliated home care aide is 18 or older, is employed by [AGENCY NAME], and is listed on the Home Care Aide Registry (HSC §1796.12(a)). An aide does not have direct client contact until cleared on the registry (HSC §1796.43(a)). Aides are employees, not independent contractors, for purposes of [AGENCY NAME]'s license.

DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (B2(a)): The aide provides non-medical home care services as defined in HSC §1796.12(n), as listed on the client's service plan, for a client who cannot perform them because of age or disability. These include:
- bathing, dressing, feeding, exercising, and personal hygiene and grooming;
- transferring, ambulating and positioning;
- toileting and incontinence care;
- assisting with self-administered medication (within the limits below);
- housekeeping, meal preparation and laundry;
- transportation, shopping, correspondence and phone calls;
- companionship.
In addition, the aide:
(1) arrives on time, uses the visit verification system, and records the services delivered;
(2) follows the service plan and does not substitute other tasks without the supervisor's approval;
(3) reports changes in the client's condition, incidents, safety hazards and any client complaint to the Client Care Supervisor right away (SVC-07, SAF-02);
(4) calls 911 in an emergency and then notifies the supervisor (SVC-07);
(5) reports suspected abuse, neglect or exploitation as a mandated reporter (PER-02);
(6) respects client rights, privacy and property and follows the conduct rules (CR-01, CR-03, PER-08);
(7) follows infection control practices (SAF-03);
(8) completes entry-level training before client contact and annual training thereafter (TRN-02, TRN-03).

LIMITS (what the aide may not do): The aide may not assist with any medication that would otherwise require administration or oversight by a licensed health care professional (HSC §1796.12(n)). The aide does not provide medical or skilled nursing care (PRG-03, SVC-04).

QUALIFICATIONS AND CONDITIONS: Age 18 or older; employed by [AGENCY NAME]; fingerprint (Live Scan) and registry clearance and registry listing before direct client contact; TB clearance; completion of required training; ability to perform the physical duties of the position with or without reasonable accommodation (PER-01, PER-03, PER-05).

LINES OF SUPERVISION (B2(b)):
- Supervision received: the aide reports to the Client Care Supervisor (JD-02), and ultimately to the Administrator or Designee (JD-01).
- Supervision given: none.

This policy meets HCS 281 (8/15), p.14, item B3(b) — Personnel Policies, Hiring Practices: documentation must include informing employees that conditions of their employment include fingerprint clearance, statement of prior criminal convictions, TB clearance, and registration on the Home Care Aide Registry.

CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT: [AGENCY NAME] informs every applicant in writing that employment is conditional on all four of the following, and that failure to satisfy any one is grounds for withdrawing the offer or ending employment:
(1) FINGERPRINT CLEARANCE through Live Scan and the CDSS background check process;
(2) a STATEMENT OF PRIOR CRIMINAL CONVICTIONS, completed on form LIC 508;
(3) TB CLEARANCE under HSC §1796.45 — an examination 90 days before employment or within 7 days after hire for aides hired on or after January 1, 2016, repeated at least every 2 years while negative; the exam is a condition of both initial and continuing employment;
(4) REGISTRATION on the Home Care Aide Registry, with the aide cleared and listed before any direct client contact.

OTHER HIRING STANDARDS: Affiliated home care aides must be 18 or older and employed by [AGENCY NAME]. Any staff person, volunteer or employee who will have contact with clients, prospective clients or confidential client information must satisfy the background clearance requirements before that contact or access (HSC §1796.43(a)(1)). [AGENCY NAME] hires without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability or other protected status (CR-05). [AGENCY NAME] will give CDSS, on request, a complete list of its affiliated aides and proof that each meets the Act's requirements for registry, training and TB clearance (HSC §1796.37(a)(5)).

TB RULES: The exam is a CDC-recommended, FDA-licensed TB infection test and, if positive, a chest X-ray. An aide with a positive test may not work unless [AGENCY NAME] holds documentation from a licensed medical professional that the aide poses no risk of spreading disease. The aide submits, and [AGENCY NAME] keeps on file, the practitioner's certificate after each exam. After a positive test has been followed by an X-ray, repeat exams are no longer required. A transferring aide meets the requirement with a certificate from within the past 2 years, or with verification from the prior HCO that it holds the certificate and a copy is provided before the aide starts.
